=============================================================
What an inode is
=============================================================
An inode stores metadata about a file, but not the file’s name or its data.
Each file has one inode, and that inode has a unique number within the filesystem.

=============================================================
What an inode contains
=============================================================
An inode typically stores:

File type (regular file, directory, symlink, etc.)
File size
Owner (UID) and group (GID)
Permissions (rwx)
Timestamps
atime (last access)
mtime (last modification)
ctime (last metadata change)
Number of hard links
Pointers to the data blocks on disk

=============================================================
What an inode does not contain
=============================================================
❌ File name
❌ Directory path

👉 File names live in directories, which map:
filename → inode number


Simple example
ls -li
Output:
123456 -rw-r--r-- 1 user user 1024 Jan 30 file.txt

Here:

123456 = inode number
file.txt = name stored in directory
inode 123456 holds all metadata + data block pointers

=============================================================
Why inodes matter (real-world)
=============================================================

=============================================================
1. Disk full but space still left 😵
=============================================================

You can run out of inodes even if disk space is available.
df -h    # disk space
df -i    # inode usage


Common with:
Millions of tiny files (logs, cache, temp files)

=============================================================
2. Hard links
=============================================================
Multiple filenames can point to the same inode:
ln file1 file2

Both names share:
Same inode number
Same data
File is deleted only when link count = 0.

=============================================================
3. Deleting open files
=============================================================
If a file is deleted but still opened by a process:

Directory entry removed
Inode stays until process closes it
This explains “disk space not freeing” issues.
Directories and inodes
A directory is also a file
It contains a list of:
filename → inode number

Inode stores metadata, not filename or data.
Filename is a mapping to an inode inside a directory.
Multiple filenames can point to one inode (hard links).
Disk can be full due to inode exhaustion.
